    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Concrete takes about 28 days to reach its design strength, but you can usually walk on it after 24 to 48 hours and drive a passenger car on it after about 7 days, when typical mixes have reached roughly 70 percent of their final strength. Heavy vehicles such as moving trucks, RVs and concrete trucks should generally wait the full 28 days. Those numbers assume mild weather around 70 degrees Fahrenheit and a slab kept moist in its first week; cold weather can stretch every milestone, and hot, windy weather can weaken the surface if it dries too fast. Curing is a chemical reaction, not drying, and that difference explains most of the advice below.
        </p>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. When Can You Walk, Drive and Build on New Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The standard reference point for concrete strength is 28 days. Mix designs are specified by the compressive strength they reach at 28 days, such as 3,000 or 4,000 psi, and testing labs break cylinders at that age to check them. That does not mean the slab is soft for four weeks. Concrete gains strength quickly at first and more slowly later, so most everyday uses are safe long before day 28.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Milestone</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Time (mild weather)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Initial set, surface finished</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A few hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Finishers work it while still plastic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light foot traffic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Avoid dragging furniture or pets with claws</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Remove slab edge forms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longer in cold weather; follow contractor</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Passenger cars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 70% of design strength</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy vehicles, RVs, trucks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 28 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Design strength reached</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Most sealers on new concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly about 28 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check product label; some allow sooner</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Walking is fine after 24 to 48 hours for most slabs, as long as you avoid scuffing the surface. That means no dragging chairs, no wheelbarrows, no bicycles skidding and no pets with sharp claws running across it in the first few days. Patio furniture can usually go on after about a week; heavy planters, hot tubs and grills are better placed after the slab has had more time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Passenger cars are generally fine after about 7 days in mild weather, because typical concrete has reached roughly 70 percent of its design strength by then. Drive slowly, avoid turning the wheels while stopped, and keep tires off the edges of the slab, which are the weakest areas. Heavy vehicles, such as moving trucks, delivery trucks, RVs, or another concrete truck for a neighboring pour, should wait until the slab has reached design strength at about 28 days.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Key Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before foot traffic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before passenger cars</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Design strength</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~70%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Strength at 7 days, typical</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Always defer to the contractor's instructions. They know the mix, the weather on pour day and whether accelerators or other admixtures were used. A high-early-strength mix may be ready sooner; a cold-weather pour may need longer. If you poured it yourself, assume the slower end of every range unless the weather was ideal and you kept the slab moist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curing-vs-drying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Curing the Same as Drying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, and the difference matters. Concrete hardens through hydration, a chemical reaction between cement and water that forms the crystals binding the sand and stone together. Hydration needs water to continue. If the slab dries out too soon, hydration slows or stops near the surface, and the concrete there never reaches its potential strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why curing means keeping the concrete moist and at a reasonable temperature, not letting it dry. The goal in the first days is to hold water in the slab so the reaction can continue. A slab that dries fast on a hot, windy day can end up with a weak, dusty surface that scales, crazes or wears prematurely, even if the concrete beneath is sound.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drying is a separate, much slower process that matters mostly when something will be applied over the concrete. Fresh concrete holds a lot of water beyond what hydration uses, and it takes weeks to months to dry out. Flooring installers often use a rough rule of thumb of about a month per inch of thickness under good conditions, and they test moisture rather than relying on the calendar before laying flooring or coatings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strength gain continues long after 28 days, though at a much slower rate. Well-cured concrete may continue to gain some strength for months. The 28-day figure is simply the industry's agreed benchmark for when the concrete should have reached its specified strength, which is why sealer makers and engineers use it as a reference point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do You Keep New Concrete Moist?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Moist curing for at least the first seven days is a common recommendation for ordinary concrete, and the first three days matter most. There are three practical approaches: add water, cover the slab to hold water in, or apply a curing compound that forms a membrane. Each works if done properly, and the best choice depends on your schedule and weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wet curing means keeping the surface damp, usually by misting or gently sprinkling it several times a day, or by covering it with wet burlap kept damp. It produces excellent results but requires attention, because letting the slab dry out between waterings undercuts the benefit. Start once the surface is hard enough not to be marked by the water, and avoid washing out the finish with a strong spray.
          </p>

          <p>
            Covering the slab with plastic sheeting traps moisture and is easy for homeowners. Lay the plastic flat, overlap the seams and weigh down the edges so wind does not lift it. Plastic can cause some uneven discoloration on colored or decorative concrete, so contractors sometimes avoid it on those finishes and use other methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Curing compounds are sprayed or rolled on right after finishing and form a membrane that slows evaporation. They are convenient and widely used by contractors. Some are cure-and-seal products that also act as a first coat of sealer. Others must be removed or allowed to wear off before a different sealer or coating is applied, so check compatibility if you plan to seal later.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Keep it wet, not dry</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For the first week, your job is to stop water leaving the slab. Damp burlap, plastic sheeting or a curing compound all work; letting the sun and wind dry it out is the one thing that does not.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Does Temperature Change Curing Time?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Temperature has a big effect on how fast concrete gains strength. Hydration speeds up in warm weather and slows in cold. Many contractors consider roughly 50 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit ideal. Below about 50 degrees, strength gain slows noticeably, and every milestone in the timeline above can stretch significantly. Near freezing, hydration nearly stops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freezing is the real danger. If fresh concrete freezes before it has gained enough strength, ice forming in the pores can permanently damage it. Industry guidance commonly calls for protecting new concrete from freezing until it reaches a minimum early strength, often cited as around 500 psi, which typically takes a couple of days in mild conditions and longer in cold. Contractors use insulated blankets, heated enclosures, accelerating admixtures and warm mix water to get there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot weather brings different problems. Above about 90 degrees, concrete sets faster, giving finishers less time to work it, and the surface can lose water quickly, especially in wind, low humidity or direct sun. Rapid surface drying causes plastic shrinkage cracks and a weaker top layer. In hot weather, contractors often pour early in the morning, use set retarders, shade or windbreaks, and start curing immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain is another concern on pour day. Light rain after the surface has been finished and has begun to set is usually not a problem and can even help curing. Heavy rain on fresh, unfinished concrete can wash out cement paste, ruin the finish and weaken the surface. Our guide to <a href="/can-you-pour-concrete-in-the-rain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pouring concrete in the rain</a> covers when to postpone and how to protect a fresh slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway: in cool weather, add time to every milestone. In cold weather, ask the contractor how the slab will be protected. In hot weather, start curing immediately and keep it moist. And regardless of weather, keep deicing salts off new concrete during its first winter, since young concrete is especially vulnerable to salt-related scaling. Our <a href="/does-salt-damage-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">does salt damage concrete</a> guide explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="forms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Can You Remove the Forms?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical slab on the ground, such as a patio, sidewalk or driveway, the wooden edge forms can usually be removed after about 24 to 48 hours in mild weather. The forms on a slab are not carrying weight; they just hold the edges in shape while the concrete sets. Once the concrete is firm enough that the edges will not slump or chip when the boards come off, the forms have done their job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold weather lengthens that wait. In cool conditions, concrete gains strength more slowly, and edges stripped too soon are easy to chip or break. Leaving forms on longer also gives some protection to the edges, which are the most vulnerable part of a new slab. If in doubt, leave them another day; there is rarely any benefit to rushing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Structural forms are a different matter. Forms for walls, columns, elevated slabs, beams and steps can carry significant load, and the timing for removing them, especially for supporting shores, depends on engineering requirements and strength testing. For anything structural, follow the engineer's or contractor's instructions, not a general rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you strip forms, pry gently and pull the boards away from the concrete rather than levering against the edge. Check the edges for honeycombing or voids, which can be patched while the concrete is still young. Then backfill soil against the edge of the slab once it has had a few more days to gain strength, sloping the soil away so water drains away from the concrete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Can You Seal New Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most sealer manufacturers recommend waiting about 28 days before sealing new concrete, but check the label, because the answer depends on the product. Fresh concrete releases moisture as it cures and dries. A film-forming sealer applied too soon can trap that moisture, leading to a cloudy or white appearance, poor adhesion or peeling. Waiting lets enough moisture escape for the sealer to bond and perform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some products are designed for newer concrete. Cure-and-seal products are applied right after finishing to help curing and act as a first coat of protection. Some penetrating sealers, such as certain silane or siloxane products, allow application sooner than film-formers because they do not form a surface film. Read the label carefully, and if a product says to wait 28 days, wait 28 days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing is especially worthwhile in freeze-thaw climates where deicing salts will be used, and for decorative concrete such as stamped or colored slabs. A good penetrating sealer reduces water and salt absorption, which slows scaling and spalling. Film-forming sealers enhance color and give a sheen. Our tested picks for the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete sealer</a> cover both types, and our <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to seal a concrete driveway</a> guide walks through prep and application.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before sealing, the concrete should be clean and dry. A simple test is to tape a square of plastic sheeting to the surface and check it after a day; if moisture collects underneath or the concrete darkens, wait longer. Also check the weather: most sealers need a dry surface, mild temperatures and no rain for at least several hours after application.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once you have applied the sealer, it needs its own drying and curing time before foot traffic and vehicles. That is separate from the concrete's cure, and it varies by product. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-sealer-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long concrete sealer takes to dry</a> covers typical times for penetrating and film-forming sealers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mistakes-timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Can You Tell If Concrete Did Not Cure Properly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Surface problems are the most common sign. A slab that dried too fast or froze early may develop a dusty surface that rubs off on your hand, a pattern of fine surface cracks called crazing, or flaking and scaling, where thin layers of the surface peel away, often after the first winter. These problems usually affect the top fraction of an inch, not the whole slab, but they are hard to fix once they appear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plastic shrinkage cracks, which are short, often parallel cracks that appear within hours of placement, usually mean the surface lost water too fast during finishing, typically in hot, dry or windy weather. Random cracks that cross the slab instead of following control joints may point to joints that were cut too late or spaced too far apart, or to a poor base, rather than curing alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tire marks, ruts or chipped edges in the first weeks suggest the slab was used too soon. Edges are the weakest part of a young slab, so cars driving over them early, or forms pried off against them, often cause chips. Avoid these by following the timeline and keeping traffic away from edges in the first month.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you see these signs, talk to the contractor promptly, since some issues may be covered by their warranty. Minor surface wear can sometimes be refreshed with a resurfacer once the slab is fully cured, and sealing helps protect a slab against further scaling. Deep or widespread problems may need professional evaluation. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-prevent-concrete-spalling-in-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">preventing concrete spalling in winter</a> covers how to protect concrete through its first cold season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the slab dry out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Curing is a chemical reaction that needs water. A slab left to bake in sun and wind in its first days can end up with a weak, dusty surface that scales later. Keep it moist with damp burlap, plastic sheeting or a curing compound for at least the first several days, ideally a week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on it too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A passenger car on concrete that is only a few days old can leave ruts, crack the slab or break the edges. Wait about 7 days in mild weather, longer in cold, and keep tires away from edges. Heavy trucks and RVs should wait about 28 days, when the slab reaches design strength.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing too early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Film-forming sealers applied to fresh concrete can trap moisture and turn cloudy, white or peel. Many manufacturers call for about 28 days of curing before sealing. Some penetrating and cure-and-seal products allow earlier application, but read the label and do the plastic sheet moisture test before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring before a freeze</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete that freezes before reaching a minimum early strength can be permanently damaged. If freezing temperatures are expected in the days after a pour, the slab needs insulated blankets or other protection. If you cannot protect it, reschedule; the cost of a ruined slab far exceeds a short delay.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Salting new concrete the first winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Young concrete is especially vulnerable to deicing salts, which add freeze-thaw stress and can cause scaling. Many concrete guides recommend avoiding deicers on new concrete through its first winter. Shovel promptly and use sand for traction instead, and consider a penetrating sealer once the concrete is ready.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can concrete cure in 24 hours?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concrete sets and hardens enough to walk on carefully within about 24 to 48 hours, but it is far from fully cured. Curing continues for weeks, with typical mixes reaching around 70 percent of design strength by 7 days and design strength by about 28 days. High-early-strength mixes and accelerators speed the early stages, but no standard mix is fully cured in a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I water new concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common recommendation is to keep new concrete moist for at least the first seven days, with the first three days most critical. Mist or sprinkle it several times a day, keep wet burlap damp, or cover it with plastic sheeting. If the contractor applied a curing compound, additional watering may not be needed. Ask what they used before you start spraying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete cure faster in hot weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It gains strength faster in warm weather, but very hot weather causes problems. Above about 90 degrees Fahrenheit, concrete sets quickly and the surface can dry out before it cures, leading to cracks and a weak top layer. In hot weather, start curing immediately, keep the slab moist and shade it if possible, rather than relying on heat to speed it up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put furniture on my new patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light patio furniture can usually go on after about a week in mild weather, as long as you place it rather than drag it. Wait longer for heavy planters, grills, fire pits and hot tubs, ideally until the slab is near design strength at about 28 days. Use pads under metal feet to avoid scratching the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it rains on new concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Light rain after the surface has been finished and has begun to set is usually harmless and can help curing. Heavy rain on fresh, unfinished concrete can wash out the cement paste, leave pits and weaken the surface. If rain is likely within a few hours of the pour, contractors cover the slab with plastic or reschedule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a concrete driveway take to cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A concrete driveway follows the same timeline as other slabs. Walk on it after 24 to 48 hours, drive passenger cars on it after about 7 days in mild weather, and keep heavy vehicles off until about 28 days. Cold weather can stretch each milestone. Keep it moist for the first week and avoid deicing salts during its first winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does thicker concrete take longer to cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The strength timeline is driven mostly by the mix and temperature, so a 6-inch slab follows roughly the same 7-day and 28-day milestones as a 4-inch slab. Thicker concrete does take longer to dry out, which matters before flooring or coatings go on. Very thick structural pours also generate more internal heat and need specific curing plans from an engineer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

