        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Concrete is ready for foot traffic in 24 to 48 hours, passenger cars in about 7 days and heavy vehicles at about 28 days, when it reaches design strength. Those milestones stretch in cool weather, and the slab only reaches its potential if it is kept moist in the first week and protected from freezing.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Treat the first month as the slab's most important period. Keep it moist, keep traffic off the edges, skip deicing salts the first winter, and wait for the sealer label's go-ahead, commonly around 28 days, before sealing. A slab cured properly will repay that patience with decades of service.
          </p>
